PANTHEON / LOOM-001 — A first falsifiable multiscale perception experiment

Status: Executed toy simulation; reproducible exploratory evidence, not a deployed system, scientific validation, or battlefield model.

Why this exists

To test a deliberately small part of the CHIMERA → PARALLAX → LOOM → ARGUS loop:

• FPV: Each of six agents receives one noisy binary signal.
• Relational: Agents can share one-hop observations through a communication graph.
• Bird’s-eye: An observer records the true state, agent judgments, disagreement, network topology, and changes over time.
• Counterfactual: Hold underlying world trajectories constant while changing the cognition and communication rules.

The hidden state is a fresh binary fact each step. Two agents have visual-like sensors, two acoustic-like, and two thermal-like. At step 120 of 240, visual reliability becomes anticorrelated with truth, while other sensors improve:

|Modality|Steps 0–119|Steps 120–239|
|--------|----------:|------------:|
|Visual  |0.84       |0.22         |
|Acoustic|0.66       |0.71         |
|Thermal |0.67       |0.86         |

These are synthetic binary observation streams, not real RGB/thermal/acoustic image processing. No environmental movement, object manipulation, navigation, military operations, or physical sensors are simulated here.

Factors

Communication topology: isolated (0 edges), chain (5), hub (5), split (6), fully connected (15). Each edge carries two one-hop directed readings each step. The network is static in LOOM-001.

Decision policy:

• Equal: positive equal votes from all visible signals, no learning.
• Fixed: learns per-modality reliability before the change; freezes it afterward.
• Adaptive: continues updating reliability from post-decision feedback.
• Oracle: knows the current sensor reliabilities; performance reference only.

Each policy/topology pair is tested on the same 200 seeded worlds. A correct judgment is worth 1, incorrect 0, and an exactly tied vote 0.5, approximating randomized ties without additional noise.

The ground truth is revealed to every agent after every decision, allowing supervised calibration from sensors available locally or from immediate neighbors. This simplification is essential: LOOM-001 tests belief revision, not how real agents discover truth without labels.

Results (200 seeds)

Late = mean accuracy from steps 180–239 (60 steps after the change), averaged across all six agents and 200 trials.

|Topology|Messages/step|Fixed|Adaptive|Oracle|
|--------|------------:|----:|-------:|-----:|
|Isolated|0            |58.6%|77.8%   |78.0% |
|Chain   |10           |57.1%|84.3%   |84.6% |
|Hub     |10           |53.7%|81.1%   |82.0% |
|Split   |12           |58.3%|87.4%   |87.7% |
|Complete|30           |44.0%|94.2%   |94.5% |

The complete graph has identical information for all six agents and hence zero agent disagreement even when their unanimous decision is wrong. Thus complete connectivity + outdated estimates can generate confident collective error. More connectivity alone did not guarantee better decisions.

Comparison limitations: The topologies have different signal counts per agent and communication costs. This experiment does not isolate pure topology effects from signal availability, sensor composition, or graph placement. The current scalar observation streams are independent conditional on the hidden truth; real sensors may have correlated errors. The fixed policy is intentionally trained only in the original regime. The oracle has privileged regime knowledge.

Reproduce

Requires Python 3.10+ standard library only.

python pantheon_loom_001.py --selftest
python pantheon_loom_001.py --trials 200 --out .

Artifacts generated by the experiment:

• loom_001_runs.csv: all 4,000 paired simulation conditions.
• loom_001_summary.json: configuration and averaged results.
• loom_001_trace.json: a step-by-step example of six agent decisions in the chain with adaptive cognition.

The next experimental round: LOOM-002

Test whether it is the structure of the web—not just extra observations—that matters.

1. Keep communication bandwidth and degree sequence controlled; rewire which agents connect.
2. Add common-mode/correlated sensor failures and misleading communication.
3. Allow truth feedback only intermittently or not at all; infer changes through environmental consequences.
4. Vary reporting delays and information staleness.
5. Introduce active sensing, exploration actions, and sensor selection costs.
6. Compare local, group, and overhead observers with a shared, time-stamped causal record.
7. Challenge each apparent insight with counterfactual runs and unfamiliar map-generating rules.

Primary hypothesis: under equal information and communication budgets, connectivity pattern and adaptive belief revision interact to determine resilience to environmental regime changes.

Pantheon translation / relationships

• CHIMERA: generates world regimes and agent cognition.
• PARALLAX: compares local and overhead views (don’t leak overhead truth into the decisions).
• LOOM: represents one-hop information webs, costs, and changes.
• DELTA OBSERVATORY: measures the change in policy performance and disagreement before/after regime shifts.
• INDIGO HAMMER: attacks apparent findings with controls and perturbations.
• ARGUS: future reverse engineering of an effective fused representation into a human interface; not validated by LOOM-001.
• ARK: stores compact seed, executable reproduction, provenance, failure modes, and confidence.

Preserved principle (conditional, supported only in this simulation): More available information can magnify an incorrect model’s impact as well as magnify the value of adaptive learning.

Do not infer: More connectivity is harmful in general; a synthetic sensing system has been invented; real-world battlefield lessons have been validated; simulated posterior confidence equals calibrated real-world certainty.